#!/usr/bin/env bash
# power-failsafe -- anti-lag watchdog for this laptop.
#
# WHAT IT WATCHES -- THE CPU, AND NOTHING ELSE (2026-10-26, requested)
#   Two facts, both read fresh every sample:
#
#     1. CPU UTILISATION, from /proc/stat's aggregate "cpu " line, as the
#        delta between two samples: how much of the machine was actually
#        busy, not how much was queued.
#     2. CPU LAG. This is normally /proc/pressure/cpu "some avg10" -- the
#        share of time some task wanted a core and could not get one -- but
#        PSI alone is NOT a usable lag signal on a POWER-CAPPED machine, and
#        that is not a theory: measured 2026-10-26 at 17:20:41 the machine
#        was at 94.4% busy with all cores at 727-900 MHz under the 20 W cap,
#        and PSI read 10-12%. Every thread got scheduled; they simply ran at
#        a third of their speed, so nothing queued and PSI stayed quiet while
#        the desktop visibly lagged. A clock check is what sees that.
#
#   So "lagging" is now the OR of two hardware facts:
#
#       tasks stalling    PSI cpu some avg10 >= CPU_LAG (default 20%), OR
#       cores throttled   fastest core at <= CLOCK_LINE (80% of base clock)
#                         AND PSI cpu some avg10 >= CPU_LAG_FLOOR (default 8%)
#
#   and Performance is raised only when the CPU is pegged AND one of those
#   holds, for CONSECUTIVE samples. A machine that is merely busy at full
#   clock -- compiling, encoding, a game -- without a stall and without being
#   throttled is not the case this exists for, and neither is anything that
#   is not the CPU.
#
# WHY THE THROTTLE BRANCH NEEDS A STALL FLOOR (2026-11-04)
#   A slow clock under saturation is not lag, and treating it as lag is what
#   this branch did until today. Measured three times in one afternoon --
#   13:16:45, 14:30:17, 15:31:55 -- the trigger fired at 95-99% busy with the
#   fastest core at 1300-1400 MHz, PSI 3%, the GPU at 0% and the desktop not
#   lagging at all; each time it pulled Performance (120/160 W, fans up) and
#   the owner asked why. What was running was ordinary background load --
#   browser, editor, emulator, this agent -- saturating 32 threads under the
#   balanced 35/45 W cap. An all-core load clocks down there BECAUSE the cap
#   is doing its job, and with PSI at 3% nothing was waiting on a core at all.
#
#   So the clock is a THROTTLE signal and PSI is the LAG signal, and the
#   throttle branch only escalates when a stall is on top of it. The 20 W eco
#   incident this branch was built for -- 94.4% busy, PSI 10.3%, 727-900 MHz
#   -- still clears the 8% floor with room to spare, so the rescue it exists
#   for is untouched; what stops is the case where the machine is honestly
#   busy at the cap it was told to hold.
#
#   CPU_BUSY is 90%, not 98%. Measured with real work (not a `yes` burn): a
#   vitest run plus the Android emulator on this 32-thread chip peaked at
#   94.4% -- a handful of idle helper threads is enough to keep the aggregate
#   off 98 -- so a 98% line is a threshold that silently never fires.
#
# WHAT IT USED TO WATCH, AND WHY THAT WAS WRONG
#   It fired on /proc/pressure memory and io too (mem >= 30 %, io >= 50 %),
#   and on the discrete GPU being busy at all -- any draw over ~15 W or a
#   client in P0. On THIS machine that meant firing for things that are not
#   the CPU being pegged:
#
#     * io sits at 11-17 % for hours at idle (Brave reading tabs, the shell
#       writing state), so against a 12 % line the watchdog was permanently
#       in pain: 43 raises and 41 stand-downs in six hours, each pair
#       flipping the profile through power-autoswitch (two pkexec calls) and
#       firing a critical notification;
#     * four 4K NVENC streams keep the CPU idle and the GPU at P0, so a
#       video export raised the CPU and kept it raised while the card -- not
#       the CPU -- was the thing doing the work.
#
#   The owner's rule now: the CPU spiked to 100 % and started lagging, or
#   nothing. Everything else is left alone, including the GPU.
#
# WHAT IT DOES WHEN THE CPU IS PEGGED AND LAGGING -- ALARM ONLY BY DEFAULT
#   Nothing is switched, raised or unlocked. It logs the offenders and says so
#   on screen:
#     1. journal line naming the trigger and the top processes
#     2. notify-send   (a real notification)
#     3. qs -c ii ipc call failsafe flash …  (bar blink + corner card)
#
#   The power escalation this used to do -- performance profile, 120/160 W cap,
#   turbo and a raised power cap -- is still there behind FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1, but
#   it is OFF by default on purpose (2026-10-26, owner's rule): a 100% CPU
#   event is a thing to NAME and REMOVE, not a thing to power through. Raising
#   the ceiling turns a spike into heat, fan noise and battery drain and leaves
#   the actual cause untouched; the cause here was a leftover vitest run and a
#   headless emulator, which no amount of watts fixes.
#
# WHY IT CANNOT FIRE ON A BURST -- the fail-safe ladder, all of which must
# hold before it touches anything:
#
#   * BOTH conditions must exceed their line for CONSECUTIVE samples
#     (default 2 x 2 s = 4 s of sustained peg AND stall), not one bad
#     window. Retimed 2026-10-27: at 3 x 5 s the ladder needed 15 s of
#     sustained peg, but every real spike on this machine dies in 4-10 s --
#     the journal showed streaks dying at 1/3 and 2/3 all evening with no
#     raise since 19:57, so the machine crawled through every lag episode
#     on the 20 W eco cap. 4 s at >=90 % WITH a stall on top is still not
#     a page load.
#   * A minimum COOLDOWN between actions, so a flapping machine cannot get
#     profile flips every COOLDOWN.
#   * Every decision is logged to the journal (systemd user service), so
#     "why did it fire" is always answerable.
# The two lines, calibrated on this machine (32 hardware threads, measured
# 2026-10-26 with `yes` burns while sampling both numbers):
#
#     one thread per core (32) -> busy 98-99 %, lag  2-4 %   (pegged, NO lag)
#     two per core       (64)  -> busy 99-100 %, lag  9-14 %  (queueing starts)
#     four per core      (128) -> busy 99-100 %, lag 14-31 %  (real stalling)
#     idle                     -> busy 1-3 %,   lag  0-2 %
#
# So 100 % busy on its own is NOT lag -- a compile filling every core stalls
# nobody -- and the lag line has to sit above the one-thread-per-core case by
# enough that only genuine oversubscription trips it. 20 % is inside the 4x
# band and well clear of the 1x band.
CPU_BUSY=${CPU_BUSY:-90}        # overall CPU utilisation that counts as pegged
CPU_LAG=${CPU_LAG:-20}          # PSI cpu "some avg10" that counts as lagging
# The throttle branch's own floor, so a slow clock alone cannot escalate:
# see the 2026-11-04 note in the header.
CPU_LAG_FLOOR=${CPU_LAG_FLOOR:-8}   # PSI cpu "some avg10" the throttle branch also needs
# THE THROTTLE LINE IS DERIVED FROM THE CHIP, NOT HARD-CODED (2026-10-26).
#
# It was 1200 MHz, calibrated on the 20 W eco cap where the fastest core sat
# at 727-900. Measured again the same evening at 98.1% busy on the balanced
# cap: fastest core 1400 MHz, PSI 3.5% -- so BOTH lag signals read "fine" and
# the rescue could not fire at any load. 1400 MHz on an this CPU (base
# 2200, turbo 5800) is the machine being held back, but it is not below a
# number that was picked for a different cap.
#
# So the line is a fraction of /sys/.../policy0/base_frequency -- "the
# fastest core cannot even reach CLOCK_PCT of its base clock" is throttling
# on any profile, and it re-calibrates itself when the cap changes. 80% of
# 2200 = 1760 MHz here. An explicit CLOCK_LINE in the environment still wins.
CLOCK_PCT=${CLOCK_PCT:-80}      # fastest core below this % of base clock = throttled
CLOCK_LINE=${CLOCK_LINE:-}
if [ -z "$CLOCK_LINE" ]; then
    _base=$(cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/base_frequency 2>/dev/null)
    case "$_base" in
        ''|*[!0-9]*) CLOCK_LINE=1200 ;;
        *) CLOCK_LINE=$(( _base / 1000 * CLOCK_PCT / 100 )) ;;
    esac
    unset _base
fi
# 2026-10-26 17:20:41, the incident all of this comes from: 94.4% busy, PSI
# 10.3%, 727-900 MHz, profile power-saver (20 W PL1, turbo off) while the AC
# was plugged in. Nothing queued -- everything just ran at ~800 MHz.
CONSECUTIVE=${CONSECUTIVE:-2}   # samples BOTH must persist (x INTERVAL)
INTERVAL=${INTERVAL:-2}         # seconds between samples
COOLDOWN=${COOLDOWN:-300}       # min seconds between actions
RESTORE_QUIET=${RESTORE_QUIET:-90}  # calm seconds before backing out of performance

# Set the moment WE raise performance; cleared the moment WE stand down.
# Only our own engagement auto-restores -- a profile the user picked by hand
# is theirs until they change it.
engaged=0
engaged_at=0
quiet_since=0

count=0
last_action=0

log() { printf '[power-failsafe] %s\n' "$*" >&2; }

notify() {
    # $1 = -i <icon>, $2 = title, $3 = body (getopt-free: the only caller is
    # this script, so a tiny shift-based parse is enough).
    command -v notify-send >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
    local icon="applications-engineering"
    if [ "$1" = "-i" ]; then icon=$2; shift 2; fi
    notify-send -u critical -a "power-failsafe" -i "$icon" "$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null || true
}

blink() {
    # $1 = flash kind ("fire" performance engaged, "green" stood down,
    # "error" generic trouble), $2 = the sentence. Best-effort: the shell may
    # be reloading or gone; never let this fail the action itself. Direct IPC
    # only -- routing through shell.sh would risk launching a whole second
    # shell just to show a blink.
    #
    # The pgrep guard is not paranoia: a `qs -c ii ipc call` client that
    # cannot immediately find the instance can fall back to SPAWNING the
    # config -- and a second full shell instance segfaults on this build
    # (2026-10-01: an orphaned backgrounded IPC client did exactly that and
    # popped the crash dialog). The shell check filters zombies: a Z-state
    # process still matches a plain pgrep. The shell must be alive AND not a
    # zombie for the blink to be worth attempting.
    # The shell process is named `qs` on this build (`qs -c ii`), NOT
    # `quickshell`. The guard only looked for the long name, so it never found
    # the running shell and every blink was skipped with "shell not alive" --
    # a notification this daemon printed while the desktop it wanted to warn
    # was plainly on screen. Both names are accepted, because either could be
    # the launcher's choice after an update.
    local alive=0 pid stat
    for pid in $(pgrep -x qs; pgrep -x quickshell); do
        stat=$(ps -o stat= -p "$pid" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')
        # Zombies start with Z; anything else (S, R, S<, SNl, ...) is live.
        [ -n "$stat" ] && [ "${stat:0:1}" != "Z" ] && alive=1
    done
    [ "$alive" = "1" ] || { log "shell not alive — skipping blink"; return 0; }
    qs -c ii ipc call failsafe flash "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    return 0
}

# PSI "some" avg10, as a whole percent. File format is
#   some avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=...
#   full avg10=...
# Split on [= ] the pair "avg10=0.00" becomes TWO fields ("avg10", "0.00"),
# so the loop matches the literal token and prints its successor -- matching
# /^avg10=/ matches nothing, and the whole detector reads 0 forever. The line
# selector is the literal "some" ($1): each /proc/pressure/<res> file only
# describes its own resource.
psi_some() {
    awk -F'[= ]' '$1 == "some" { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "avg10") { print $(i+1); exit } }' "/proc/pressure/$1" 2>/dev/null | cut -d. -f1
}

# ── CPU utilisation, from two /proc/stat samples ────────────────────────────
# /proc/stat's first line is the aggregate:
#   cpu  user nice system idle iowait irq softirq steal guest guest_nice
# "busy" is the total minus idle AND iowait: time spent waiting on a disk is
# not the CPU being pegged (and io is no longer a trigger of its own, so
# counting it as busy would smuggle it back in).
cpu_sample() {
    awk '/^cpu / { idle = $5 + $6; total = 0; for (i = 2; i <= NF; i++) total += $i; printf "%d %d\n", idle, total }' /proc/stat
}

read -r cpu_prev_idle cpu_prev_total < <(cpu_sample)
read -r cpu_prev_idle cpu_prev_total <<<"${cpu_prev_idle:-0} ${cpu_prev_total:-0}"

# THE SAMPLE STATE MUST NOT LIVE IN A SUBSHELL (fixed 2026-10-26).
#
# This used to be `busy=$(cpu_busy)` with cpu_busy echoing its result. A
# command substitution runs the function in a SUBSHELL, so the two globals it
# assigned (cpu_prev_idle / cpu_prev_total) were thrown away every sample and
# the window never advanced: every reading was "busy since the daemon
# STARTED", not "busy in the last INTERVAL". On a daemon that had been up for
# hours that number only ever fell towards the machine's idle average -- so
# `busy -ge 90` was false during a genuine 100% spike and the whole watchdog
# was dead code. Proven by running it by hand under a 31-thread burn: 98% busy
# logged within 2 s of start (window = its whole life, all load), while the
# systemd instance logging the same burn stayed silent.
#
# So the function now sets CPU_BUSY_NOW in the CURRENT shell and is called
# without $(...). Same numbers, real window.
cpu_busy_now() {
    local idle total di dt
    read -r idle total < <(cpu_sample)
    di=$((idle - cpu_prev_idle))
    dt=$((total - cpu_prev_total))
    cpu_prev_idle=$idle
    cpu_prev_total=$total
    CPU_BUSY_NOW=0
    [ "$dt" -gt 0 ] || return
    # Guard against a counter reset / a shorter window than one tick.
    [ "$di" -ge 0 ] && [ "$di" -le "$dt" ] || return
    CPU_BUSY_NOW=$(( (100 * (dt - di)) / dt ))
}

top_offenders() {
    # Whose fault it is, measured over the last second -- NOT `ps -o pcpu`,
    # whose number is an average over the process's whole lifetime. On this
    # machine that average belongs to whatever has been running longest (the
    # Android emulator at ~53 %, the shell at ~31 %), so an alarm built on it
    # named the wrong processes while the actual spike -- five vitest workers
    # at ~95 % -- never appeared. Two /proc snapshots, one second apart.
    local n=${1:-5}
    python3 - "$n" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null
import glob, os, sys, time

def snap():
    d = {}
    for p in glob.glob("/proc/[0-9]*"):
        try:
            raw = open(p + "/stat", "rb").read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
        except OSError:
            continue
        e = raw.rfind(")")
        if e < 0:
            continue
        f = raw[e + 2:].split()
        try:
            d[p.rsplit("/", 1)[1]] = (raw[raw.find("(") + 1:e], int(f[11]) + int(f[12]))
        except (IndexError, ValueError):
            continue
    return d

a = snap()
t0 = time.time()
time.sleep(1.0)
b = snap()
elapsed = max(time.time() - t0, 0.001)
hz = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")
rows = []
for pid, (name, ticks) in b.items():
    if pid in a:
        pct = 100.0 * (ticks - a[pid][1]) / hz / elapsed
        if pct >= 5:
            rows.append((pct, name, pid))
rows.sort(reverse=True)
print("; ".join(f"{n} {p:.0f}% (pid {i})" for p, n, i in rows[:int(sys.argv[1])]) or "nothing above 5%")
PY
}

cur_freq_mhz() {
    # The FASTEST core, because "the machine is throttled" must not be
    # claimed just because one parked core sits low. Every policy is read;
    # unreadable cpufreq means "unknown", which is reported as a high number
    # so the decision falls back to PSI instead of guessing "throttled".
    local f max=0 v
    for f in /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy*/scaling_cur_freq; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || continue
        v=$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null) || continue
        case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*) continue ;; esac
        v=$((v / 1000))
        [ "$v" -gt "$max" ] && max=$v
    done
    [ "$max" -gt 0 ] && echo "$max" || echo 99999
}

desired_mode() {
    # The user's own power mode, as the shell's config records it. This is the
    # source of truth for the CPU profile, NOT the profile PPD happens to be
    # on: the shell re-asserts this key (services/PowerMode.qml) whenever the
    # config re-signals, so any profile this daemon writes that disagrees with
    # it is put back within the minute. Reading it here is what lets the
    # failsafe raise the machine without stealing the setting.
    python3 - <<'PY' 2>/dev/null || echo default
import json, os
p = os.path.expanduser("~/.config/illogical-impulse/config.json")
try:
    with open(p) as f:
        cfg = json.load(f)
except Exception:
    print("default"); raise SystemExit
print(cfg.get("battery", {}).get("powerMode", "default"))
PY
}

apply_performance() {
    # $1 = the streak length that fired (for the notification's "for how long")
    # $2 = busy % at the moment it fired, $3 = the reason sentence
    local streak=$1 busy=${2:-?} lag=${3:-?}
    # The user's setting wins. If the config already says performance, there is
    # nothing here to raise: the profile is theirs and the boost is on, so
    # engaging would only mean standing down later -- which is exactly the "it
    # jumped back to power saving" this used to cause.
    if [ "$(desired_mode)" = "performance" ]; then
        log "cpu pegged (${busy}% busy, ${lag}% lag, ${streak} samples), but the user's mode is already performance — no action"
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    # Already raised and still holding: a long compile stays pegged for its
    # whole run, so without this the ladder would re-fire every COOLDOWN,
    # re-notifying each time about a boost that is already on. Nothing to do
    # is the correct answer here.
    if [ "${engaged:-0}" = "1" ] \
        && [ "$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null)" = "performance" ]; then
        log "still pegged (${busy}% busy, ${lag}% lag, ${streak} samples), boost already on — holding"
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    log "cpu pegged (${busy}% busy, ${lag}, ${streak} samples): reporting"

    # Default: name the spike, change nothing about the hardware. See the
    # header -- escalation is opt-in, so a heavy build never turns into a
    # 120/160 W, fans-up, turbo-on event behind the owner's back.
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_ESCALATE:-0}" != "1" ]; then
        local top
        top=$(top_offenders 5)
        log "alarm only, no hardware change (set FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1 to escalate). top: ${top:-unknown}"
        notify -i "speed" "CPU pegged and lagging" \
            "${busy}% busy -- ${lag}. Top: ${top:-unknown}"
        blink fire "CPU pegged — ${busy}% busy, ${lag}"
        # Remember we reported it so the same spike does not re-notify every
        # COOLDOWN while it lasts, and stay un-engaged so stand_down never
        # tries to undo a change we did not make.
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    # DRY-RUN exists so the trigger can be PROVEN on a real spike without
    # touching the machine: engaging normally converges the CPU cap through
    # pkexec, which can raise an authentication dialog in the middle of
    # whatever the owner is doing. With FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN=1 the decision and
    # its reason are logged and nothing is written.
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY-RUN would set the CPU to performance"
        engaged=1
        engaged_at=$(date +%s)
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=$(date +%s)
        return 0
    fi
    powerprofilesctl set performance 2>/dev/null \
        && log "cpu profile -> performance" \
        || log "WARN: powerprofilesctl set performance failed"

    # Converge the CAP now instead of waiting for power-autoswitch's next 5 s
    # poll. Measured 2026-10-26: without this the profile was performance at
    # 17:28:56 but the package limit was still 20/30 W with turbo off until
    # 17:29:00 -- five seconds of the spike spent in the cap this rescue exists
    # to lift. Same call the shell's power-mode switch makes, so the cap keeps
    # exactly one writer and one policy table.
    if [ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/power-autoswitch" ]; then
        "$HOME/.local/bin/power-autoswitch" --converge 2>&1 | sed 's/^/[converge] /' >&2 || true
    fi
    engaged=1
    engaged_at=$(date +%s)
    quiet_since=0
    notify -i "local_fire_department" "Performance mode engaged" \
        "CPU pegged at ${busy}% for ${streak}x${INTERVAL}s -- ${lag}. Switched CPU to Performance."
    blink fire "Power failsafe: CPU pegged and lagging — switched to Performance"
    last_action=$(date +%s)
}

stand_down() {
    # Back out of OUR OWN performance engagement: sustained calm (every one
    # of the last RESTORE_QUIET seconds below both lines), profile still on
    # performance (do not fight a user who has already moved it), Dynamic
    # Boost off (it idles at ~5-8 W but it is not ours to keep raising).
    log "calm for ${RESTORE_QUIET}s: standing down"
    # What it returns to is the USER'S MODE, not a hardcoded power-saver. The
    # shell re-asserts battery.powerMode, so writing power-saver here over a
    # user who picked balanced or performance is undone a minute later anyway --
    # and getting that wrong is the whole reason this daemon looked like it was
    # "jumping back". If the mode IS performance, there is nothing of ours to
    # undo and the correct move is to change nothing at all.
    local mode target
    mode=$(desired_mode)
    case "$mode" in
        performance) target=performance ;;
        eco)         target=power-saver ;;
        *)           target=balanced ;;
    esac
    if [ "${FAILSAFE_DRY_RUN:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        log "DRY-RUN would stand down to ${target}"
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=0
        return 0
    fi
    if [ "$target" = "performance" ]; then
        log "user mode is performance — leaving the profile and the boost as they are"
        engaged=0
        quiet_since=0
        last_action=0
        notify -i "local_fire_department" "Performance mode is yours" \
            "Machine has been quiet for ${RESTORE_QUIET}s. Leaving the CPU profile exactly as your Performance mode set it."
        blink green "Failsafe: CPU calm — Performance mode is yours"
        return
    fi
    if [ "$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null)" != "$target" ]; then
        powerprofilesctl set "$target" 2>/dev/null \
            && log "cpu profile -> $target (the user's mode: $mode)" \
            || log "WARN: powerprofilesctl set $target failed"
    else
        log "cpu profile already $target (the user's mode: $mode)"
    fi
    engaged=0
    quiet_since=0
    # The cooldown guards rapid fire-fire repeats, but a stand-down needed 90
    # s of sustained calm to happen at all -- pressure coming back after that
    # is genuinely new load, and the CONSECUTIVE-sample streak is the guard
    # against blips. Leaving last_action set here would blind the re-raise
    # for up to a full COOLDOWN after the restore (raise at t=0, calm, restore
    # at t=95, pressure at t=100 -> 200 s of lag with no action). Reset it.
    last_action=0
    notify -i "check_circle" "Performance no longer needed" \
        "CPU has been calm for ${RESTORE_QUIET}s. Back to ${mode}."
    blink green "Failsafe: CPU calm — back to ${mode}"
}

log "watching the CPU only: fires after ${CONSECUTIVE} samples at >=${CPU_BUSY}% busy AND (>=${CPU_LAG}% PSI lag OR (<=${CLOCK_LINE} MHz AND >=${CPU_LAG_FLOOR}% PSI), interval ${INTERVAL}s, cooldown ${COOLDOWN}s"
log "ignoring memory, io and GPU: they are not the CPU being pegged"
if [ "${FAILSAFE_ESCALATE:-0}" = "1" ]; then
    log "action: ESCALATE (opt-in) -- performance profile; auto-restore after ${RESTORE_QUIET}s calm"
else
    log "action: ALARM ONLY -- log, notify and blink; no profile, cap or turbo change (FAILSAFE_ESCALATE=1 opts in)"
fi

while true; do
    cpu_busy_now
    busy="$CPU_BUSY_NOW"
    lag=$(psi_some cpu)
    lag=${lag:-0}

    freq=$(cur_freq_mhz)
    # Pegged AND (stalling OR throttled). See the header: PSI cannot see lag on
    # a power-capped chip, because a slower core still schedules everyone --
    # the desktop crawls while the kernel honestly reports no queueing.
    hot=0
    if [ "$busy" -ge "$CPU_BUSY" ]; then
        if [ "$lag" -ge "$CPU_LAG" ]; then
            hot=1; why="tasks stalling (${lag}% PSI)"
        elif [ "$freq" -le "$CLOCK_LINE" ] && [ "$lag" -ge "$CPU_LAG_FLOOR" ]; then
            # Throttled AND stalling. The clock on its own is not lag: an
            # all-core load running at the cap it was given clocks down on
            # purpose and stalls nobody (2026-11-04, see the header).
            hot=1; why="cores throttled (${freq} MHz, line ${CLOCK_LINE}) with ${lag}% PSI"
        fi
    fi

    if [ "$hot" = "1" ]; then
        count=$((count + 1))
        # A pegged sample restarts the calm window: RESTORE_QUIET promises
        # EVERY one of the last 90 s below both lines, so a spike in the
        # middle of the quiet stretch must push the stand-down out, not
        # sail past it on the strength of the first calm second.
        quiet_since=0
        log "cpu pegged ${busy}% busy, ${why} (${count}/${CONSECUTIVE})"
        if [ "$count" -ge "$CONSECUTIVE" ]; then
            now=$(date +%s)
            if [ $((now - last_action)) -ge "$COOLDOWN" ]; then
                apply_performance "$count" "$busy" "$why"
            else
                log "still pegged, but cooling down ($((COOLDOWN - (now - last_action)))s left) — no action"
            fi
            count=0
        fi
    else
        # One calm sample resets the streak: bursts must be consecutive.
        [ "$count" -gt 0 ] && log "no longer pegged (${busy}% busy, ${lag}% lag, ${freq} MHz), streak reset"
        count=0
        # ── Auto-restore: stand down after sustained calm ──
        if [ "$engaged" = "1" ]; then
            now=$(date +%s)
            [ "$quiet_since" = "0" ] && quiet_since=$now
            profile=$(powerprofilesctl get 2>/dev/null || echo "?")
            if [ "$profile" != "performance" ]; then
                log "profile is '$profile' (not ours anymore) — will not auto-restore"
                engaged=0
            elif [ $((now - quiet_since)) -ge "$RESTORE_QUIET" ]; then
                stand_down
            fi
        else
            quiet_since=0
        fi
    fi
    sleep "$INTERVAL"
done
